# State Engineering

> "A state is not data you carry. A state is the minimum information required to uniquely determine every future decision."

---

# Why State Exists

Many optimization problems explode because multiple execution paths eventually reach situations that are fundamentally identical.

Instead of solving these identical situations repeatedly, we represent them using a **state**.

The purpose of state is to uniquely describe the future.

---

# Definition

A **State** is the minimum complete information required to determine every future legal decision from the current point onward.

It is **not**

- the entire history
- the path taken
- the answer accumulated so far

It is only the information that changes the future.

---

# Fundamental Principle

Whenever solving a problem, ask:

> "If I remove this piece of information, will the future become different?"

If YES

↓

It belongs in the state.

If NO

↓

It does not belong in the state.

---

# State vs Search Value

This is one of the most important distinctions.

## State

Determines future possibilities.

Examples

- Position
- Speed
- Remaining Fuel
- Keys Owned
- Stops Used

---

## Search Value

Measures how good the current path is.

Examples

- Steps
- Cost
- Time
- Probability

Search Value does **not** automatically belong in the state.

---

# Capability

Some information changes what actions are legal.

This information is called a capability.

Examples

- Keys
- Fuel
- Remaining obstacle eliminations
- Permissions
- Energy

If acquiring or consuming something changes future reachability,

it belongs in the state.

---

# Resource Types

## Consumable Resources

Examples

- Fuel
- Obstacle Eliminations

The amount decreases over time.

State Example

(row,col,remainingFuel)

---

## Persistent Resources

Examples

- Keys
- Permissions

Once acquired,

they remain forever.

State Example

(row,col,keyMask)

---

# Identity vs Quantity

Sometimes

the future depends on

"which"

resources exist,

not

"how many."

Example

Keys

Incorrect

NumberOfKeys = 3

Correct

{A,C,F}

Identity determines future behavior.

---

# State Audit

After proposing a candidate state,

perform this test.

Imagine two executions reaching the same candidate state.

Ask

> "Can they have different futures?"

If YES

↓

State is incomplete.

If NO

↓

State is complete.

This is the most important correctness test.

---

# Minimal Complete State

A good state satisfies two conditions.

## Complete

Contains everything needed to determine the future.

---

## Minimal

Contains nothing unnecessary.

Never optimize for the smallest state.

Optimize for the **minimum complete state**.

---

# Dominance

Sometimes two executions reach the same state.

If one execution is always better,

the other can be safely discarded.

Dominance must always be proven.

Never assumed.

---

## Total Dominance

Example

Obstacle Elimination

Remaining

5

dominates

Remaining

2

because every future path available to the second execution is also available to the first.

---

## Partial Dominance

Example

Cheapest Flights

(city,stops)

Lower cost alone does not dominate.

Stops must also match.

---

## Set Dominance

Example

Keys

{A,B,C}

dominates

{A,B}

because every lock that can be opened by the smaller set can also be opened by the larger set.

---

## No Dominance

Different futures.

Example

Race Car

(position=7,speed=+8)

(position=7,speed=-8)

Future actions differ.

Neither execution dominates.

---

# State Compression

Sometimes the state can be represented more efficiently.

Examples

Key Set

↓

Bitmask

Coordinates

↓

Index

Characters

↓

Integer IDs

Compression changes representation.

It must never change meaning.

---

# Storage Strategy

Storage depends on the traversal guarantee.

---

## visited[state]

Use when the traversal guarantees the first visit is optimal.

Examples

- BFS
- Multi-source BFS

---

## bestValue[state]

Use when a better path may still appear later.

Examples

- Dijkstra
- Bellman-Ford
- Dynamic Programming

---

# State Finalization

Every traversal has a point where a state becomes final.

## BFS

Finalized when discovered.

Reason

Every edge has equal weight.

---

## Dijkstra

Finalized when popped.

Reason

A cheaper path may still exist before extraction.

---

# Engineering Process

When discovering state, follow this order.

Optimization

↓

Information that survives

↓

Candidate State

↓

State Audit

↓

Dominance

↓

Compression

↓

Traversal

↓

Storage

↓

Implementation

---

# Common Mistakes

❌ Putting Search Value inside the state

❌ Forgetting capability

❌ Adding unnecessary dimensions

❌ Compressing state incorrectly

❌ Assuming dominance without proof

❌ Choosing storage before proving traversal guarantees

---

# Recognition Signals

You probably need additional state when:

- Future legal moves depend on acquired resources.
- Previous decisions change future possibilities.
- Two executions reaching the same location behave differently.
- A path carries information that affects future actions.

Position alone is usually **not** enough.

